// The instruction students see for each step: the idea in whole sentences, then, after a blank
// line, exactly what to do. The lesson's fuller text stays folded under "Why?".
import { getLesson } from './engine.js';

const RESET = 'Write a function called `resetGame` at the bottom that puts every starting value back.';

const BRIEFS = {
  // Rewritten after the teacher found one-line versions too terse to follow.
  "t1/Meet the grid": "Your game is drawn on the blue square, called the **canvas**. It is made of tiny dots: 400 across and 400 down.\n\nThe **Grid** draws numbered lines over the canvas, like graph paper, so you can find any dot. Every dot has two numbers, its **coordinates**: `x` counts across from the left, and `y` counts **down** from the top.\n\nPress **Run**, then turn on **Grid**. Move your mouse over it and read the numbers.",
  "t1/Where your code lives": "Code is grouped into **functions**. A function is a named set of instructions. It starts at `{` and ends at `}`, and the lines between those two marks belong to it.\n\nFind `function draw() {` on line 5 and its closing `}` on line 7. Everything you draw today goes **between** those two lines.",
  "t1/How a line of code is built": "Every drawing command is written the same way. The computer only understands it when every mark is in place, so learn the marks first.\n\nLook at line 2. Find the **name** of the command, the `( )` around its numbers, the **comma** between the numbers, and the `;` that ends the line.",
  "t1/Add a sun": "`ellipse` draws a circle or an oval. It needs four numbers: `x` and `y` say **where** its middle goes, then the width and height say **how big** it is.\n\nType this line below `background`. It puts a sun near the top right corner:",
  "t1/Add the ground": "`rect` draws a rectangle. Its `x` and `y` are its **top-left corner**, not its middle. After them come its width and its height.\n\nType this line below the sun. It lays a strip of ground along the bottom of the canvas:",
  "t1/Add color": "`fill` chooses the color for every shape drawn **after** it, like dipping a brush in paint. You give it a color name inside quote marks, such as `'yellow'`.\n\nType one `fill` line above the sun and another above the ground. Then swap in other names from the color list:",
  "t1/Draw a character": "Now use what you know, with no line to copy. You can use `rect`, `ellipse`, and `line(x1, y1, x2, y2)`. Guess the numbers, press **Run**, and change them until it looks right.\n\nBuild a character that stands on the ground, from at least **three more** shapes.",
  "t1/Read the error": "Everyone makes typing mistakes, so the computer tells you where they are. Practice reading that message now, while nothing is really wrong.\n\nDelete one `)` from any line and press **Run**. Read the message under your game, then put the `)` back.",
  "t3/Why draw() matters": "Your sketch has two functions. `setup()` runs **once**, when the program starts. `draw()` runs again and again, about 60 times every second, and paints the whole picture each time.\n\nPress **Run** and watch the saucer. It stays still, because `draw()` paints it in the same spot every time.",
  "t3/A name for a number": "To move the saucer, the computer has to remember where it is. A **variable** does that job: it is a name that holds a value.\n\nType this line at the very top, above `setup`. It makes a variable named `saucerX` and puts 50 in it:",
  "t3/Use the name": "A variable does nothing until you use it. Wherever you write `saucerX`, the computer swaps in the value it holds, which is 50 right now.\n\nThe saucer is two shapes, and each one has a `50` for its `x`. Replace both of those `50`s with `saucerX`:",
  "t3/One name, one change": "Both shapes now get their `x` from the same variable, so one change moves them both. See it for yourself.\n\nChange the `50` in your `let` line to `300` and press **Run**. Watch the whole saucer jump. Then change it back to `50`.",
  "t3/See what it holds": "You cannot see what a variable holds unless you ask the computer to show you. `text` draws words on the canvas, so use it to print the value.\n\nType these lines at the end of `draw()`, just above its closing `}`:",
  "t3/Change it every frame": "Now make the value change while the program runs. In code, `=` means **\"becomes\"**. This line says: `saucerX` becomes its old value plus 2.\n\nType it at the end of `draw()`. `draw()` runs 60 times a second, so watch the number climb and the saucer fly:",
  "t3/Try removing background()": "Why does `draw()` start with `background` every time? Find out by turning that line off.\n\nType `//` in front of `background` and press **Run**. The saucer leaves a smear, because the old pictures are never painted over. Then delete the `//`.",
  "t3/Your own variable": "Now do all three steps yourself. Make a variable with `let` at the top, use it in a shape, and change it at the end of `draw()`.\n\n**Make**, **use** and **change** a second variable for a new moving thing, such as a falling meteor.",
  "t4/Variables p5 gives you": "Last lesson you made `saucerX` and changed it yourself. p5 also has variables that it makes and changes **for you**. `mouseX` and `mouseY` always hold where the mouse is.\n\nReplace the body's two position numbers with `mouseX` and `mouseY`, so the creature follows your mouse:",
  "t4/Do math in the arguments": "The body follows the mouse now, but the eyes stay behind. Anywhere a number goes you can write math, such as `mouseX + 20`, and the computer works it out.\n\nChange the two eyes so each sits 20 dots to one side of the mouse and 10 dots above it:",
  "t4/Check the mouse with if": "An `if` lets your program make a choice. It asks a question inside its `( )`, and runs the code inside its `{ }` **only when the answer is yes**.\n\nType these lines at the end of `draw()`. They open the creature's mouth while the mouse button is held down:",
  "t4/Show a number on screen": "p5 changes `mouseX` and `mouseY` many times a second. Print them on the canvas so you can watch it happen, the same way you printed `saucerX`.\n\nType these lines at the end of `draw()`. Then move the mouse and watch the two numbers:",
  "t4/Your creature, your rules": "Now use the mouse for an idea of your own. `mouseY` could set a size. `mouseX` could even set a color: in place of a name, a color can be three numbers for red, green and blue, each from 0 to 255.\n\nMake one more thing in your picture change when the mouse moves, by using `mouseX` or `mouseY` in a **new** place.",
  "a1/A speed variable": "A number with a name can be changed in one place. If every arrow key uses `speed`, one change retunes your whole game.\n\nMake a variable called `speed`, under your other variables at the top, and set it to 3.",
  "a1/Walk in four directions": "`keyIsDown()` checks whether a key is being held, and it checks every frame. Each arrow key changes `playerX` or `playerY` by `speed`. Remember that up means a **smaller** `y`.\n\nType this above `// player`, then press **Run** and click the canvas so it can hear your keys:",
  "a1/Stay in the world": "Right now your hero can walk off the edge of the map. `constrain(value, low, high)` keeps a number between a lowest and a highest value.\n\nAbove `// player`, use `constrain` on both `playerX` and `playerY` so the hero stays on the map.",
  "a1/Add a pond": "Now the world can change the player. `dist()` gives the distance between two points, here the player and the pond's center. If that is less than 70, the player is in the water and slows down. `else` handles being out of the water.\n\nType this above `// movement`:",
  "a1/Pick your world": "Is this a forest, a desert, or the ocean floor? You decide what kind of world your hero walks through.\n\nChange the colors and add some scenery with shapes. Draw it above `// player` so the player appears on top.",
  "a2/Describe a wall": "A wall is a rectangle, so it needs four numbers: where it is, how wide it is, and how tall it is. Variables give those numbers names.\n\nMake variables named `wallX`, `wallY`, `wallW`, and `wallH` at the top. Then draw the wall with `rect` above `// player`.",
  "a2/Remember where you were": "Here is the trick for solid walls: **save the old position**, move, then undo the move if you hit something. These two variables are only needed during this frame, so they live inside `draw()`.\n\nType this above `// movement`. It saves where the player is before they move:",
  "a2/Check for overlap": "Two rectangles overlap only when four things are all true. The player's left is before the wall's right, and its right is after the wall's left. The same goes for top and bottom. `&&` joins the four checks, so every one must be true.\n\nType this above `// wall`. If the player overlaps the wall, it undoes the move:",
  "a2/Slide along walls": "Walk diagonally into the wall and you stick to it. That happens because the code undoes both directions at once. It is better to undo only the `x` move, then check again. Only if you still overlap does it undo `y`.\n\nReplace the two undo lines, `playerX = oldX;` and `playerY = oldY;`, with this:",
  "a2/Build a maze": "One wall is not much of a maze. A second wall needs its own four variables, its own `rect`, and its own overlap check.\n\nAdd a second wall using a new set of variables, and try to make the player go a certain way. If writing it all twice feels clumsy, arrays will solve that soon.",
  "a3/Game variables": "A **boolean** is a variable that holds only `true` or `false`. That fits a question like: do you have the key? `hasKey`, `won`, and `lost` all start as `false`. The other variables hold the score and where the key, door, and treasure are.\n\nType these at the top, after `let wallH = 220;`:",
  "a3/Pick up the key": "`!` means **not**, so `!hasKey` reads as not hasKey. It is true until you have the key. The key is drawn only while that is true. Touching it sets `hasKey` to `true` and adds 10 to `score`.\n\nType this above `// player`:",
  "a3/A door that checks": "The door blocks you unless you have the key. It uses the same overlap check and undo trick as your wall, inside `if (!hasKey)`. Once you have the key, the door is not drawn and cannot stop you.\n\nType this above `// player`:",
  "a3/Lava pit": "A game needs something to fear. `lost` is the boolean that remembers the player has lost. `dist` can tell when the player is close enough to touch a round shape.\n\nAbove `// player`, draw a round lava pit at (100, 200). Use `dist` to set `lost` to `true` when the player touches it.",
  "a3/The treasure": "The treasure is the prize behind the door. Winning means setting `won` to `true`. It needs two things to be true at once, and `&&` joins two checks so both must be true.\n\nAbove `// player`, draw the prize at `treasureX` and `treasureY`. The player wins only with the key **and** when close to it.",
  "a3/Show what happened": "The game knows the score and whether you won or lost, but the player cannot see any of it. `text()` puts words on the screen. The starter code shows the score and leaves two messages for you to write.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**, then finish the lines marked TODO. Then try to win without the key.",
  "a4/Write a resetGame function": "A **function** is a named set of instructions that you write once and call whenever you need it. Yours will let the game begin again. Look at your variables and decide which ones change during play.\n\nWrite a function called `resetGame` at the bottom that puts every starting value back.",
  "a4/Press R to restart": "p5 calls a function named `keyPressed()` once for each key press. That makes it a good place to restart the game. Inside it, `key` tells you which key was pressed.\n\nWrite your own `keyPressed()` at the bottom. It should call `resetGame()` when `key === \"r\"`.",
  "a4/Your first for loop": "A **for loop** repeats code while counting. It has three parts: where to start, when to keep going, and what to change each time. This one starts `x` at 0 and adds 40 each time. Each time around, it draws one tree.\n\nType this above `// pond`. It grows a line of trees across the bottom of the map:",
  "a4/Loop with math": "`%` gives the **remainder**, which is what is left over after dividing. It wraps big numbers back onto the canvas, so the pattern looks random but is not. Try `(i * 97) % 400` for `x` and `(i * 53) % 400` for `y`.\n\nBelow `background`, use a for loop to scatter 40 small flowers.",
  "a4/Your scenery": "A loop can draw many things from a few lines of code. The numbers in the loop decide how many there are and where each one sits.\n\nUse a loop to add scenery that fits your world: rocks, cacti, seaweed, or torches. Change the numbers in the loop and watch the pattern change.",
  "a5/Meet objects": "An **object** bundles related values under one name, like `{ x: 150, y: 60 }`. An **array** is an ordered list inside `[ ]`. Together they make a list of walls. Each wall is one object with its own `x`, `y`, `w`, and `h`.\n\nReplace your four wall variables, `wallX` to `wallH`, with this:",
  "a5/Draw them all": "`walls.length` is how many items the list has. `walls[0]` is the **first** one, because counting starts at 0. The loop visits each wall in turn and names it `w`. A dot reads a value from an object, so `w.x` is that wall's `x`.\n\nReplace your old `rect(wallX, wallY, wallW, wallH);` line with this:",
  "a5/A helper that returns an answer": "You will need that long overlap check again and again, so put it in a function. `return` hands an answer back from a function. Loop over `walls` and return `true` if a player at that position overlaps one. If the loop finishes without a hit, return `false`.\n\nWrite a function called `hitsWall(x, y)` at the bottom that answers whether a player there overlaps any wall.",
  "a5/Use your helper": "Your bump check still uses the old wall variables. `hitsWall` can do that job for every wall. Test one direction at a time so the player still slides. First call `hitsWall(playerX, oldY)` and undo `x` if it hits. Then call `hitsWall(playerX, playerY)` and undo `y` if it hits.\n\nReplace your whole bump check, starting at `// bump into the wall`, so it uses `hitsWall`.",
  "a5/Design your maze": "A new wall now costs one line, not four variables and another check. Each wall is one object with `x`, `y`, `w`, and `h`. Keep the two walls beside the door, because they seal off the treasure.\n\nAdd at least one more wall to your `walls` array, one line each. Check that the key can still be reached.",
  "a6/Build the gem list": "The gem list will change as the game runs, because collected gems are removed. If a function makes the list, a restart can call it again and bring every gem back. `return` hands the list back from the function.\n\nWrite a function called `makeGems()` at the bottom that **returns** a list of at least four `{ x, y }` objects.",
  "a6/Create the list": "Your function can build the gems, but nothing calls it yet. The game needs a variable to hold the list that the function hands back.\n\nCreate a variable called `gems` at the top, under your other variables, and fill it by calling your function.",
  "a6/Loop backward and remove": "`splice(i, 1)` removes 1 item at position `i`. Removing an item slides everything after it down a place, so a forward loop would skip the next gem. Looping **backward**, from the last item to the first, avoids that bug.\n\nType this above `// player`. It draws each gem and removes any gem the player touches:",
  "a6/Fix the restart": "Restart the game now and the gems you collected stay gone. This is why the list comes from a function: each call to `makeGems()` returns a full list.\n\nHave `resetGame` fetch a fresh list of gems by calling your function.",
  "a6/Require all gems to win": "The treasure chest should count only once every gem is collected. Then the list is **empty**, so `gems.length` is 0. `===` asks whether two things are exactly equal.\n\nAdd one more condition to your treasure `if`, using `&&`, so the player wins only when the list is empty.",
  "a6/Show progress": "Players want to know how much is left to find. `gems.length` is how many gems are still in the list, and `text()` can put that number on the screen.\n\nUnder your score line, show how many gems remain, using `text()` and `gems.length`. Then spread gems around your map.",
  "a7/A Gem class": "A **class** is a blueprint for making many objects that all work the same way. A **method** is a function that belongs to an object. The constructor takes `x` and `y`. `show()` draws the gem. `touches()` **returns** whether the player is within 22 dots of it.\n\nWrite a class called `Gem` at the bottom, with `show()` and `touches()` methods.",
  "a7/Draw a map with letters": "You can paint a map with letters and let a program build the world. Each string is one row, and each character is a 40-by-40 tile. `#` is wall, `g` is a gem, and `.` is open ground. Rows and columns like this are called **2D** data.\n\nType this map at the very top of the file:",
  "a7/Build it with nested loops": "A loop inside a loop visits every tile. The outer loop picks a row, and the inner loop walks across it. `tileMap[row][col]` reads one character. Multiplying by 40 turns tile numbers into pixels. `push` adds a wall or a `Gem` to the end of its list.\n\nType this function at the bottom:",
  "a7/Use the class": "The gems are `Gem` objects now, so each one can draw itself and check itself. You call a method with a dot, like `g.show()`.\n\nIn your gem loop, replace the drawing line and the `dist` check so each gem handles itself, using `g.show()` and `g.touches()`.",
  "a7/Plug it in": "`buildMap()` does nothing until it is called. It must run once when the game starts, and again on every restart so the gems come back.\n\nCall `buildMap()` in `setup()`, and again in `resetGame()` in place of `gems = makeGems();`.",
  "a7/Paint your own map": "The map is now something you can draw with the keyboard. Change a letter and the world changes with it.\n\nMove the letters around to design your world. Move your key, door, and treasure to fit it too. Check that the starting spot, top left, is not inside a wall.",
  "a8/How BFS thinks": "Picture water poured on your map. It spreads to every neighboring tile, then their neighbors, in rings. The first time it touches the goal, it has followed a **shortest path**. That is **breadth-first search**. It uses a **queue**, a list where you add to the back and take from the front, like a lunch line.\n\nRead this over, and talk it through with a partner before moving on.",
  "a8/Which tiles are open?": "The search needs to know which tiles it may enter. A tile off the map is not open, and neither is a wall. `||` means **or**, so one `if` can check all four edges of the map.\n\nWrite a function called `isOpen(col, row)` at the bottom. It returns `false` for a tile that is off the map or a wall.",
  "a8/Write the search": "This is the heart of it. The search starts at the player and spreads out until it reaches the hunter. `cameFrom` remembers which tile led to which, so the hunter can follow it home. Three key lines are left for you. They are what make this a **queue**.\n\nPress **Add the starter code** and read every comment. Then finish the three lines marked TODO.",
  "a8/Create the hunter": "The hunter walks from tile to tile. So it remembers its current spot, `x` and `y`, and where it is headed, `targetX` and `targetY`. A restart must send it back to where it began.\n\nCreate an object called `hunter` near the top with those four values, all set to 380. Then set them back to 380 in `resetGame`.",
  "a8/Make it hunt": "When the hunter reaches its target tile, it asks your search where to go next. Between tiles, it glides 1.5 dots a frame. If it touches the player, `lost` becomes `true`.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**, then finish the two TODOs. Change the speed to set the difficulty.",
  "a8/Outsmart it": "An **algorithm** is a set of exact steps that solves a problem. Yours finds a shortest path through any maze, so it keeps working when the maze changes.\n\nPlay, and lure the hunter into long routes around your walls. Then change your map and watch the hunter find the new shortest path without a change to its code.",
  "s1/A speed variable": "Your ship needs a speed: how far it moves each frame. If the speed has a name, you can retune the whole game later by changing one number.\n\nBelow `let playerSize = 40;`, make a **variable** called `shipSpeed` and set it to 5.",
  "s1/Fly left and right": "`keyIsDown()` asks whether a key is being **held** right now. `draw()` asks it every frame, so the ship keeps gliding for as long as you hold an arrow key.\n\nEach `if` changes `playerX` by `shipSpeed`. Subtracting moves the ship left, and adding moves it right.\n\nType this above `// player`. Then press **Run** and click the canvas so it can hear your keys:",
  "s1/Stay on the screen": "Right now the ship can fly off the edge and vanish. `constrain(value, low, high)` fixes that. It keeps a number between a lowest and a highest value.\n\nThe lowest `playerX` should be 0. Work out the highest, so the whole ship still fits on the canvas.\n\nAbove `// player`, use `constrain` on `playerX` so the ship stops at both edges.",
  "s1/Flickering engine": "`random` gives a different number each time it is used. Use it inside `draw()` and you get a new number every frame, which makes a shape **flicker**.\n\nA flame is a good use for that. A `triangle` needs three points, and its tip can move.\n\nAt the end of `draw()`, draw a triangle under the ship. Use `playerX` and `playerY` so it follows, and `random` for its tip.",
  "s1/Pick your mission": "Your ship does not have to be a spaceship. It could be a dragon over a city, or a submarine firing bubbles. A **theme** makes the game yours.\n\nChoose a theme and change the colors to match. Then add a shape or two to your ship. Use `playerX` and `playerY` in each new shape so it moves with the ship.",
  "s2/Bullet variables": "A bullet needs a position, so it gets an `x` and a `y`. The game also has to know whether a bullet exists right now.\n\nA **boolean** does that job. It is a variable that holds only `true` or `false`. `bulletActive` starts as `false`, because nothing has been fired yet.\n\nType these lines at the top, below `let shipSpeed = 5;`:",
  "s2/Pull the trigger": "`keyPressed()` is a special function. p5 runs it once each time a key goes down, so one press fires one bullet.\n\n`&&` means **and**, and `!` means **not**. So the `if` reads: the key is space and no bullet is active. Then the bullet starts at the ship and turns on.\n\nType this at the very bottom, below the end of `draw()`:",
  "s2/Send it flying": "The bullet exists now, but nothing moves it or draws it. That should only happen while `bulletActive` is `true`.\n\nRemember that up means a **smaller** `y`. So a bullet flies up when you subtract from `bulletY` every frame.\n\nAbove `// player`, add an `if` that checks `bulletActive`. Inside it, move the bullet up and draw it as a thin `rect`.",
  "s2/End of its life": "Fire once, then try again. Nothing happens. The bullet is still active far above the screen, so the game thinks you already have one.\n\nA bullet has a **lifecycle**: it is created, it travels, and it is removed. Yours is missing the last stage.\n\nBelow the bullet's `rect` line, set `bulletActive` to `false` when the bullet passes the top of the screen.",
  "s2/Count your shots": "A **counter** is a variable you add 1 to each time something happens. Counting your shots proves the firing code runs exactly once per press.\n\nThe counter starts at 0 at the top. It goes up where the bullet is fired, and `text` shows it at the end of `draw()`.\n\nCount your shots in a variable called `shots`, and show the number on screen with `text`.",
  "s2/Tune your weapon": "Three numbers decide how your weapon feels. One is how far the bullet moves each frame, and two are its width and height. Its `fill` sets the color.\n\nChange the bullet's speed, size, and color, then press **Run** and fire. Try slow and huge, then tiny and fast. Keep what suits your game.",
  "s3/Game variables": "A game needs something to want and something to fear. So it keeps a `score`, and two booleans named `won` and `lost`. Both start `false`, and any part of your game can check them.\n\nThe enemy needs variables too: where it is, and how fast it moves.\n\nType these lines at the top, below `let shots = 0;`:",
  "s3/The enemy marches": "The enemy slides sideways, because `enemySpeed` is added to `enemyX` every frame. At each wall it turns around and steps **down**, closer to your ship.\n\n`||` means **or**. The `if` is true when either side is true: the enemy is past the right wall, or past the left one.\n\nType this above `// player`:",
  "s3/Did the bullet hit?": "`dist` gives the distance between two points. That makes a quick test for a **collision**, which means two things touching.\n\nIf the bullet is closer than 22 to the enemy's center, it is a hit. The bullet turns off and you score 10. The enemy goes back to the top and gets faster.\n\nType this above `// player`:",
  "s3/Win and lose": "Rules for winning and losing end the game. You **lose** if the enemy reaches your ship. You **win** when your score gets to 100.\n\nThink about which numbers tell you how far down the enemy and the ship are.\n\nAbove `// player`, add two `if` checks. One sets `lost` to `true`, and the other sets `won` to `true`.",
  "s3/Show what happened": "The game now knows when it is won or lost, but the player cannot see it. `text` can put the score and a message on the screen.\n\nThe starter code goes at the end of `draw()` and shows the score. Two messages are left for you.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**. Then finish the lines marked TODO, so the game says when the player wins or loses.",
  "s3/Playtest the balance": "**Balance** means tuning the numbers until a game is neither too easy nor too hard. You cannot judge that alone, because you already know your own game.\n\nPress **Run** and play. Then change the speeds and the score goal, and ask a neighbor to try it. Change the enemy's color or shape to fit your theme.",
  "s4/Write a resetGame function": "A **function** is a named set of instructions. You write it once and call it whenever you need it. Use what you learned at the Crossing to let the game begin again.\n\nLook at your variables at the top. Some change during play, like `score` and `enemyX`.\n\nAt the very bottom, write a function called `resetGame` that puts every one of those starting values back.",
  "s4/Press R to restart": "Writing a function does not run it. `resetGame` only runs when some line **calls** it, by saying `resetGame()`.\n\nYour `keyPressed()` already runs once for each key press. That makes it a good place to listen for the R key.\n\nAt the top of `keyPressed()`, add a new `if` that checks `key === \"r\"` and calls your function.",
  "s4/Your first for loop": "A **for loop** repeats code while counting. It has three parts: where to start, when to keep going, and what to change each time.\n\nThis one starts `x` at 20, keeps going while `x` is less than `width`, and adds 50 each time around. That draws a row of 8 shield blocks.\n\nType this above `// player`:",
  "s4/A scrolling starfield": "A loop can fill your sky. Count `i` from 0 up to 50, and give each star an `x` of `(i * 83) % 400`.\n\nFor `y`, use `(i * 47 + frameCount) % 400`. `frameCount` goes up by 1 every frame, so the stars move. `%` gives the remainder, which wraps a star back to the top.\n\nBelow `background`, write a for loop that draws 50 small stars with those positions.",
  "s4/Make it your sky": "The star loop is yours to change. A different color, size, or speed can turn stars into snow, bubbles, or embers.\n\nBigger, slower stars behind the fast ones are called **parallax**. It makes the sky look deep.\n\nChange the loop to fit your theme and press **Run**. Then try a second loop with bigger, slower stars.",
  "s5/Meet arrays and objects": "An **array** is a list of values inside `[ ]`. An **object** bundles values under one name, like `{ x: 10, y: 20 }`. You read one with a dot, like `b.x`.\n\nEach bullet will be an object, and one array will hold every bullet on screen. The list starts empty.\n\nReplace the three old bullet variables, `bulletX`, `bulletY`, and `bulletActive`, with this one line:",
  "s5/Add to the list": "`push` adds an item to the end of an array. Now each press of space pushes a new bullet object, with its own `x` and `y`.\n\nThe `!bulletActive` test is gone. You no longer have to wait for the last bullet to leave.\n\nIn `keyPressed()`, replace the whole `if` that fires your bullet with this:",
  "s5/Loop backward and remove": "This loop moves and draws every bullet in the list. `splice(i, 1)` removes the one at position `i` when it passes the top.\n\nRemoving an item slides everything after it down one place. A forward loop would then skip the next bullet. Looping **backward**, from the last item to the first, avoids that.\n\nReplace your old `// bullet` block, the whole `if (bulletActive)`, with this:",
  "s5/Check every bullet": "The hit test only knows about one bullet, and `bulletActive` is gone. It needs the same backward loop, so it can test every bullet in the list.\n\nOn a hit, remove that bullet with `splice`. Then use `break`, which stops a loop early. One hit is enough.\n\nRewrite your `// did the bullet hit?` block as a loop over `bullets`. The score and enemy lines stay the same.",
  "s5/Fix the restart": "Your `resetGame` still mentions `bulletActive`, a variable that no longer exists. A restart should clear every bullet off the screen instead.\n\nAn empty list is written `[ ]`, as it was at the top of your code.\n\nIn `resetGame`, replace the `bulletActive` line so it empties the `bullets` list. Then press **Run** and fire away.",
  "s6/Build a formation": "One enemy becomes six. An **array of objects** can hold them, where each enemy has its own `x`, `y`, and `speed`.\n\nA function called `makeEnemies()` will build that list. It starts an empty list, uses a for loop to `push` six objects, and then **returns** the list. To return means to hand a value back.\n\nReplace your three enemy variables with a list called `enemies`, built by `makeEnemies()`.",
  "s6/March them all": "Every enemy must move now. Loop backward over `enemies`. At the top of the loop, give the current enemy a short name such as `e`.\n\nThen do for `e.x`, `e.y`, and `e.speed` what your old code did for one enemy. Move the check for reaching the player inside the loop too.\n\nReplace your old `// enemy` block with that loop. End the loop body with the comment `// HIT CHECK GOES HERE`.",
  "s6/Every bullet, every enemy": "A **nested loop** is a loop inside a loop. For each enemy, this one tries each bullet. With 6 enemies and 10 bullets, that is 60 checks every frame.\n\nThe inner loop counts with `j`, so it does not get mixed up with `i`. A hit removes both the bullet and the enemy.\n\nReplace the `// HIT CHECK GOES HERE` comment with this. Then delete your old `// did a bullet hit?` block:",
  "s6/New win and lose rules": "Losing is now handled inside the enemy loop, so the old lose check can go. Winning needs a new rule too.\n\n`.length` says how many items an array holds. When it is 0, the list is **empty**, and every enemy has been shot.\n\nReplace your old `// win or lose` block with one rule: the player wins when the `enemies` list is empty.",
  "s6/Fix the restart": "Your `resetGame` still sets `enemyX`, `enemyY`, and `enemySpeed`. Those variables are gone, so a restart would not bring the swarm back.\n\n`makeEnemies()` returns a fresh list of six enemies every time it is called.\n\nIn `resetGame`, replace the three old enemy lines with one line that builds a fresh formation.",
  "s6/Your formation": "The formation comes from the numbers inside `makeEnemies`. They set how many enemies there are, how far apart they stand, and how fast they move.\n\nChange the spacing and speed, then press **Run**. For more rows, try a loop inside the loop in `makeEnemies`. Then make the enemies look like your theme.",
  "s7/An Enemy class": "A **class** is a blueprint for making many objects that all work the same way. You built classes at the last Crossing, and enemies are a natural fit.\n\nA **method** is a function that belongs to an object. Inside the class, `this.x` means this one enemy's own `x`.\n\nAt the bottom, write a class called `Enemy` with `x`, `y`, and `speed`, plus `update()` and `show()` methods.",
  "s7/Let enemies handle themselves": "Your `Enemy` class knows how to move and draw. The loop in `draw()` can ask each enemy to do those jobs itself.\n\nYou call a method with a dot, like `e.update()`. Two short calls can stand in for seven lines.\n\nIn your enemy loop, replace the lines that move and draw `e` with calls to its two methods.",
  "s7/Describe waves as data": "Each wave is a small object. It says how many enemies the wave has and how fast they go. The variable `wave` remembers which one you are on.\n\nThis is **data-driven design**: the rules live in a list you can edit. A new wave is one new line.\n\nReplace the line `let enemies = makeEnemies();` with this:",
  "s7/Read the data": "The game has to read its waves. `waves[wave]` picks the current one, and `wave` is the **index**, a position in the array.\n\nIn `makeEnemies`, start by saving the current wave in a variable such as `w`. Loop `w.count` times and push a `new Enemy` that uses `w.speed`.\n\nRewrite `makeEnemies` that way. Fill `enemies` in `setup()`, and set `wave` to 0 in `resetGame`.",
  "s7/Next wave!": "Clearing the screen should not end the game any more. It should bring on the next wave.\n\nWhen `enemies` is empty, add 1 to `wave`. If `wave` is still less than `waves.length`, there is another wave to build. If not, the player wins.\n\nReplace your old `// win` check: move to the next wave, or win when none is left.",
  "s7/Write your own waves": "Now the data is yours. A good game has a difficulty curve: easy, then harder, then a surprise.\n\nThe player should see which wave they are on. `wave` starts at 0, so show `wave + 1`.\n\nAdd your own lines to `waves`. Then use `text()` below the score line to show the wave number.",
  "s8/What is a state machine?": "A **finite state machine** is in exactly one **state** at a time, with clear rules for when to switch. A state is what something is doing right now.\n\nYour boss has three states. In `patrol` it moves around. In `aim` it holds still, as a warning. In `rest` you get a chance to attack it.\n\nSketch the three states on paper. Draw an arrow from each one to the next.",
  "s8/Boss data": "The boss is one object that bundles everything about it. It needs `x: 170`, `y: 40`, `hp: 20`, `state: \"patrol\"`, `timer: 0`, and `speed: 2`.\n\nIt only shows up after the final wave. So the game also needs `bossActive`, set to `false`, and an empty list called `enemyShots`.\n\nBelow `let wave = 0;`, create the `boss` object, then `bossActive` and `enemyShots`.",
  "s8/The state machine": "Each frame the boss checks its state and does only that one thing. That set of exact steps is an **algorithm**.\n\n`timer` counts frames in the current state. At 60 frames a second, 120 is 2 seconds. When time is up, the boss switches state and the timer goes back to 0.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**. The `patrol` state is finished as your example. Finish the two TODOs for `aim` and `rest`.",
  "s8/Aim with trigonometry": "`atan2` gives the **angle** from one point to another. Here that is the angle from the boss to the player. `cos` and `sin` turn an angle back into x and y movement.\n\nThe loop fires three shots. One is aimed right at the player, and two go a little to each side.\n\nType this function at the very bottom:",
  "s8/Bring on the boss": "The starter code draws the boss and its shots above `// player`. The boss flashes while it aims, as a warning. Your bullets still need to hurt it.\n\nYour wave check also has to change. It says `won = true`, but now the boss should appear instead.\n\nPress **Add the starter code** and finish the two TODOs. Then change `won = true` in your wave check to `bossActive = true`.",
  "s8/Fix the restart": "A restart must put the boss back too. If it does not, a new game starts with a hurt boss and old shots still flying.\n\nIn `resetGame`, above `wave = 0;`, set `bossActive` back to false. Then reset the boss's `hp`, `state`, and `timer`, and empty `enemyShots`.",
  "d1/Your first box": "Until now your games had two directions. **WEBGL** in `createCanvas` turns on 3D, which adds a third. In this mode, (0, 0) is the middle of the canvas.\n\nType this line below `// world`. `box` draws a 3D block, and 80 is its size:",
  "d1/Turn on the lights": "Without light, a 3D shape looks like a flat sticker. `ambientLight` lights everything a little. `directionalLight` is the sun, shining from one direction. `fill` gives the box its color.\n\nType these lines above `box(80);`:",
  "d1/Three directions": "`translate(x, y, z)` moves the spot where the next shape is drawn. `y` goes **down**, and `z` comes **toward you**. `push` saves your place and `pop` returns to it, so the move does not shift anything else.\n\nType these lines above `fill(240, 130, 90);`. They draw wide, flat ground under your box:",
  "d1/Turn the whole world": "A rotate command turns everything drawn after it. `rotateX` tips the world so you look down on it. `rotateY` turns it around. `spin` grows a little every frame, so the turning never stops.\n\nType these lines above `// ground`:",
  "d1/Add a roof": "Your box needs a roof to look like a house. A roof is one more box, moved up to sit on top. Remember that up is a **negative** `y`. Wrap the move in `push` and `pop` so nothing else shifts.\n\nBelow `box(80);`, put a wide, flat, dark red box on top of your house, using `push`, `translate`, and `pop`.",
  "d1/Build your own spot": "A world needs more than one house. `sphere(30)` draws a ball, and `cylinder(20, 80)` draws a shape like a can. Each new shape needs its own `translate` to move it into place.\n\nAdd three more shapes to your world. Wrap each one in `push` and `pop`, then press **Run** to see where it lands.",
  "d2/Few sides, flat faces": "**Low poly** art is made from a few flat faces. The last numbers in `cone` and `cylinder` set how many sides they have. Fewer sides look more low poly. `rotateX(PI)` flips the cone so its tip points up.\n\nType the `makeTree` function at the very bottom. Then type the other lines at the end of `draw()` to draw one tree:",
  "d2/Sharpen the faces": "A **model** is a 3D shape saved once and drawn again and again. `buildGeometry` turns your tree drawing into one saved shape. `FLAT` makes every face a single shade, which is the low poly look.\n\nType the first line below `let spin = 0;` and the next two below `noStroke();`. Then replace `makeTree();` in `draw()` with the last line:",
  "d2/Grow a forest": "A loop can plant 38 trees, each at a `random` spot. But `draw()` runs again and again, so the trees would jump around. `randomSeed` makes `random` give the same numbers every frame, so trees stay still.\n\nIn `draw()`, replace the four lines that draw your one tree, from `push();` to `pop();`, with `plantForest();`. Then type the function at the very bottom:",
  "d2/Clear the yard": "Trees are growing through the house. `dist` tells how far apart two spots are. The house sits at the center, where `x` and `z` are both 0. An `if` can skip any tree that is too close.\n\nIn `plantForest`, use `dist` and an `if` so a tree is only drawn when it is more than 120 from the center.",
  "d2/Add your own model": "A forest needs more than trees. Any shape can become a model the same way. Write a function that draws it, then save it with `buildGeometry` and `FLAT`. A rock could be `sphere(18, 5, 3)`.\n\nMake a second model, such as a rock, and scatter ten of them outside the yard in `plantForest`.",
  "d2/Count the faces": "**Abstraction** means keeping the details that matter and leaving out the rest. The `6` in your `cone` line is how many sides the tree has. How few can it have and still look like a tree?\n\nChange the cone's `6` to `3`, then to `40`, and press **Run** each time. Pick the smallest number that still looks like a tree.",
  "d3/Stop the spin": "A spinning world is hard to walk in. You are about to control the view yourself, so the spin has to go. Your hero also needs variables: where they stand, which way they face (`heading`), and how fast they walk.\n\nDelete the three lines from `rotateX(-0.45);` to `rotateY(spin);`. Then replace `let spin = 0;` with these lines:",
  "d3/Make your hero": "Your hero is made of two boxes. The big one is the body. The small one sticks out in front, so you can see which way the hero faces. `rotateY(heading)` turns both boxes to face that way.\n\nType the `drawPlayer` function at the very bottom. Then type `drawPlayer();` at the end of `draw()`:",
  "d3/Turn and walk": "`heading` is an **angle**: the way the hero faces. The left and right arrows change it. Walking forward can move the hero in `x` and `z` at once. `sin` and `cos` split one step into an `x` part and a `z` part, based on the heading.\n\nType the `movePlayer` function at the very bottom. Then type `movePlayer();` below `// world`:",
  "d3/A camera that follows": "`camera` decides where you watch from. It takes three spots: where the camera is, what it looks at, and which way is up. Yours sits behind and above the hero and looks at them. `perspective` sets how wide the lens is.\n\nType the `perspective` line below `noStroke();` in `setup()`. Then type the `camera` line below `movePlayer();`:",
  "d3/Stay in the world": "Right now you can walk off the edge of the world. A **boundary** is a limit on where the player can go. `constrain` holds a number between a lowest and a highest value.\n\nAt the end of `movePlayer`, use `constrain` so both `playerX` and `playerZ` stay between -165 and 165.",
  "d3/Bump into the house": "You can still walk through your house. `movePlayer` already saves where you stood in `oldX` and `oldZ` before each step. If a step lands too close to the house, you can undo it.\n\nAt the end of `movePlayer`, use `dist` to go back to `oldX` and `oldZ` when you are closer than 62 to the center.",
  "d4/A grid of heights": "A **2D array** is a list of lists, which makes a grid. Yours holds a height for every spot of land. `noise` gives smooth random numbers, so nearby spots get similar heights. `lerp` keeps the yard flat.\n\nType the first three lines at the very top. Type the `makeHills` function at the very bottom, and `makeHills();` below `noStroke();` in `setup()`:",
  "d4/Two triangles per square": "A **triangle mesh** is a surface built from many triangles. A grid square has four corners at different heights, so it may not be flat. Three points always make a flat face, so every square becomes two triangles.\n\nDelete your whole `// ground` block, down to its `pop();`. Type the two functions at the very bottom, and `drawHills();` above `ambientLight(110);`:",
  "d4/Color by height": "Each face has three corner heights, and `middle` is their average. You can use it to pick a color: grass down low, rock in between, snow up high. Remember that a smaller `y` is higher.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**, then finish the TODO so low faces are green, middle faces are rock gray, and high faces are snow.",
  "d4/Shade every face": "Real light makes steep faces darker or brighter. You can fake that with one number. `shade` compares a face's corner heights. It is 1 on flat ground, and bigger or smaller on a slope.\n\nType the `shade` line below your `middle` line. Then multiply every number in your three `fill` lines by `shade`, like this:",
  "d4/Walk on the hills": "Your hero still walks at one height, straight through the hills. The finished file gives you `heightAt`, which finds the ground height anywhere. Setting `playerY` from it after every move keeps the hero on the ground.\n\nPress **Add the finished file**. Then type this line at the end of `movePlayer`:",
  "d4/Plant on the slopes": "Your trees and rocks float or sink now, because each one is still drawn at one fixed height. `heightAt` can find the ground height at each tree's `x` and `z`.\n\nIn `plantForest`, use `heightAt` in both `translate` lines so every tree and rock sits on the ground.",
  "d5/Bake a gem": "Your world needs treasure to find. A sphere with very few sides makes a perfect gem. You save it as a model the same way you saved your tree, with `buildGeometry` and `FLAT`.\n\nType the first line below `let treeShape;` and the next two below `treeShape.computeNormals(FLAT);`. Then type the `makeGem` function at the very bottom:",
  "d5/A Gem class": "A **class** is a blueprint for making many objects. Every gem will come from one `Gem` class. Its constructor takes `x` and `z`, and sets `y` to 24 above the ground. `show()` draws the gem spinning. `touches()` returns whether the hero is closer than 34.\n\nAt the very bottom, write a class called `Gem` with a constructor, a `show()` method, and a `touches()` method.",
  "d5/Scatter in a spiral": "Twelve gems need places to sit. A spiral spreads them around the yard without any landing inside the house. The `gems` list holds them all, and `score` starts at 0.\n\nType the first two lines at the very top and the two functions at the very bottom. Then type `makeGems();` at the end of `setup()` and `showGems();` at the end of `draw()`:",
  "d5/Collect them": "Touching a gem should collect it. Removing a gem from a list shifts everything after it. So loop **backward**, from the last gem to the first, and no gem gets skipped.\n\nChange `showGems` so that when the hero touches a gem, it is removed from `gems` and `score` goes up by 10.",
  "d5/A score you can read": "Players need to see how they are doing. Text is tricky in WEBGL, so you place a normal web element over the game. `createDiv` makes that element, and `hud.html` sets the words in it.\n\nType the first line at the very top, the next four at the end of `setup()`, and the last at the end of `draw()`:",
  "d5/Beat the clock": "A **timer** turns exploring into a game. You have 90 seconds to find every gem, and **R** restarts. `won` and `lost` remember how the game ended. The hero only moves while both are false.\n\nType the three variables at the very top and the three functions at the very bottom. Then replace `movePlayer();`, add `updateClock();` above `hud.html`, and replace the `hud.html` line:",
  "d5/Make it yours": "**Game design** means choosing rules and goals that give players a reason to explore. This world is yours now, so it should feel like it.\n\nChange one thing that makes your world feel like **yours**: the colors, the shapes, or the rules. Then let a neighbor explore it.",
  "m1/What counts as AI?": "Most game enemies follow rules a programmer wrote. **Machine learning** is different: the computer finds its own rules from **examples**. You will build both kinds and see what each one is good at.\n\nTalk with a partner. Is the enemy in a video game intelligent, or is it only following rules?",
  "m1/Walk in four directions": "The player needs to move. `keyIsDown()` checks whether a key is held, and it checks again every frame. Each `if` here moves the player one way. Up means a **smaller** `y`.\n\nAfter you press **Run**, click the canvas so it can hear your keys.\n\nType this above `// player`:",
  "m1/Stay in the arena": "Right now the player can walk off the screen. `constrain(value, low, high)` keeps a number inside a range, so it can never go below `low` or above `high`.\n\nKeep the player inside the arena by using `constrain` on both `playerX` and `playerY`, above `// player`.",
  "m1/Write the bot's rules": "A **rule-based** bot looks smart because it follows `if` rules a person wrote. These four `if` statements are the bot's entire brain. Read them aloud: if I am left of the player, move right.\n\nType this above `// player`. The last two lines draw the bot:",
  "m1/Add a rule of your own": "Make the bot behave differently in some situation. For example, it could sprint when it gets close. `dist()` gives the distance between two points, like the bot and the player.\n\nAbove `// bot rules`, add a rule of your own that uses `dist()` to measure how far away the player is and changes `botSpeed`.",
  "m1/Beat your own bot": "The bot can never surprise you, because it only knows the rules you gave it. Rules only cover what their writer thought of. Could you write rules for everything a person might do?\n\nPress **Run** and play until you find a way to fool the bot every time.",
  "m2/Game variables": "A game needs to remember its score and how it ended. A **boolean** is a variable that is either `true` or `false`. `won` and `lost` start `false`, and any part of your game can check them.\n\nType these at the top, below `let botSpeed = 1.5;`. The last two say where the coin starts:",
  "m2/Grab a coin": "`dist(x1, y1, x2, y2)` gives the distance between two points. A distance under 25 means the player reached the coin. Then the score goes up by 10 and `random` moves the coin somewhere new.\n\nType this above `// player`:",
  "m2/Getting caught": "The chase needs a way to lose. `dist` can tell when two things touch, because touching things are a short distance apart.\n\nAbove `// player`, use `dist` in an `if` to set `lost` to `true` when the bot is closer than 28 to the player.",
  "m2/Winning and the score": "A game needs an ending. Winning takes 100 points, and the game should say when the player wins or loses.\n\nPress **Add the starter code** to show the score at the end of `draw`. Then finish the three lines marked `TODO`.",
  "m2/Playtest for fairness": "A game is **fair** when a new player has a real chance to win. You cannot judge that alone, because you already know every trick.\n\nAsk a neighbor to play with no hints and watch what they try. Then change `botSpeed` and the starting spots until it feels fair. Pick a theme too, like a cat and mouse, a ghost, or a robot.",
  "m3/Train your model": "Machine learning teaches a computer with examples instead of rules. Your pictures are its **training data**.\n\nOpen **Teachable Machine** and choose an Image Project with the Standard model. Make five classes named exactly `left`, `right`, `up`, `down`, and `still`. Record about 30 webcam pictures of a different gesture for each, then press **Train Model**. When the preview works, choose **Export Model**, then **Upload my model**, and copy the link.",
  "m3/Paste in your model's link": "Your game needs to know where your model is. This variable holds the link to it. If you are not ready, leave the quotes empty and the arrow keys will keep working.\n\nType this line at the top, below `let botSpeed = 1.5;`. Then paste your link between the quotes:",
  "m3/Connect your model": "Connecting a model to a game takes code you will learn later. It is already written, in a file of its own. Once it starts, a variable called `label` always holds what the model sees.\n\nPress **Add the finished file**. Then type this line inside `setup()`, below `createCanvas(400, 400);`, to start it:",
  "m3/Steer with your model": "The model's answer is text in `label`, so your code can treat it like any other input. In code, `||` means **or**: a check passes when either side is true.\n\nMake each of the four arrow checks also work when `label` names that direction.",
  "m3/Show what the model sees": "Showing the model's answer on screen helps you tell a bad model from a bug. This draws the camera picture in the corner and writes the model's `label` at the bottom.\n\nType this above `// score and messages`:",
  "m3/Test it on someone else": "A model only knows what its examples showed it. So its mistakes tell you what was missing from your data.\n\nHave a partner try your model without retraining it. Then change the lighting or the background behind you. Write down each time it fails.",
  "m4/Write a resetGame function": "To play again, the game must go back to how it began. A function called `resetGame` can put every starting value back. Look at your variables and decide which ones change during play.\n\nUse what you learned at the Crossing. Write `resetGame` at the bottom so it sets each of those variables back to its starting value.",
  "m4/Press R to restart": "p5 calls a function named `keyPressed()` once for each key press. The variable `key` holds which key it was.\n\nWrite your own `keyPressed()` at the bottom. It should call `resetGame()` when `key` is `\"r\"`.",
  "m4/Your first for loop": "A **for loop** repeats code while counting. It has three parts: where to start, when to keep going, and what to change each time. This one starts `x` at 10 and adds 40 each time.\n\nType this below `background`. It draws a row of lights along the top of the arena:",
  "m4/Loop with math": "`%` gives the **remainder** after dividing. It wraps big numbers back onto the canvas, so a pattern looks random but is not. Try an `x` of `(i * 97) % 400` and a `y` of `(i * 53) % 400`.\n\nBelow your lights, use a for loop that counts `i` up to 40 to scatter 40 small floor dots.",
  "m4/Your scenery": "One loop can draw many things, so a few lines can fill the arena with scenery. The numbers in the loop decide how many things appear and how far apart they sit.\n\nUse a loop to add scenery that fits your theme. Change the numbers and watch how the pattern changes.",
  "m5/A list of memories": "An **array** is an ordered list inside `[ ]`. Each memory will be an **object**, which bundles values under one name, like `{ x: 10, y: 20, move: \"left\" }`.\n\nType these at the top, below `let MODEL_URL`. `memories` starts as an empty list, and `lastMove` will hold the player's latest move:",
  "m5/Notice each move": "The bot can only remember moves that your code writes down. So whenever the player moves, store the direction in `lastMove` as a word.\n\nAdd one line inside each of your four movement checks. Use `\"left\"`, `\"right\"`, `\"up\"`, or `\"down\"`.",
  "m5/Record a memory": "`push` adds an item to the end of a list. `frameCount % 15 === 0` is true once every 15 frames. So this saves where the player was and what they did, but only if they moved.\n\nType this above `// bot rules`:",
  "m5/See the memories": "`memories.length` is how many items the list holds, and `memories[i]` reads one of them. A for loop can visit every memory.\n\nAbove `// player`, draw a small dot for every memory with a for loop. Then play and watch your habits appear on the floor.",
  "m5/Write nearest neighbor": "Here is the **nearest neighbor** algorithm. Look through every memory and find the one where the player was closest to where they are now. Then guess they will do the same thing again.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**, then finish the line marked `TODO` so the loop keeps the closest memory so far.",
  "m5/Let the bot use it": "Instead of chasing where you are, the bot should head for where it predicts you will be. `predictMove()` gives its guess.\n\nReplace the four chase rules. Make `targetX` and `targetY` start at the player, shift them 60 in the guessed direction, and chase that point.",
  "m6/Two ways to learn": "Nearest neighbor keeps every example and searches them each time. A **neural network** studies the examples once, during **training**. Then it answers quickly without them.\n\nPredict which one will guess your moves better. Write down your prediction and why.",
  "m6/Build and train the network": "`addData` gives the network one example: the inputs and the right answer. `normalizeData` scales the numbers so they are easier to learn from. `train` runs through the examples 30 times, and each pass is called an **epoch**.\n\nType the three variables at the top, below `let lastMove`. Type the two functions at the very bottom:",
  "m6/Press T to train": "The network learns nothing until `trainBrain()` is called. It also needs at least 20 memories to learn from.\n\nAdd a new `if` inside your `keyPressed()` that calls `trainBrain()` when the player presses T. Then collect 20 memory dots and press T.",
  "m6/Ask the network": "Asking the network takes a moment. So `classify` calls a function, `gotGuess`, when the answer is ready. We ask four times a second.\n\nType the `if` above `// remember what the player does`. Type the function at the very bottom:",
  "m6/Keep score for both": "Which algorithm is better? Count it. Each time a real move is saved, compare both guesses with `lastMove`, the move that really happened.\n\nMake `tests`, `nearestRight`, and `brainRight`, all 0, at the top. Count right guesses right before you push a memory, once `brainReady` is true.",
  "m6/Declare a winner": "**Accuracy** is how often a guess is right. Putting both scores on screen lets you compare the two algorithms with evidence. Would the winner still win with less data?\n\nShow both scores above `// score and messages` using `text()`. Then play for two minutes and compare with your prediction.",
  "m7/A Bot class": "A **class** is a blueprint that can build many objects. Your `Bot` constructor takes `x`, `y`, and `smart`, which is true or false. `update()` chases the player, using `predictMove()` only if `smart` is true. `show()` draws smart bots in a different color.\n\nWrite a class called `Bot` at the bottom, with a constructor, an `update()` method, and a `show()` method.",
  "m7/Make the rivals": "`new Bot(...)` builds one bot from your blueprint. Classes must be used after the code has loaded, which is why the bots are made in `setup()`.\n\nCreate an empty list called `rivals` at the top. In `setup()`, add one smart bot and one that is not.",
  "m7/Run the rivals": "Making the bots is not enough. Each frame, every bot has to move and be drawn, and the game must check whether it caught the player.\n\nAbove `// player`, loop over `rivals`. Ask each one to `update()` and `show()`, and set `lost` to true if it touches the player.",
  "m7/Fix the restart": "After a restart the rivals should begin in their corners again. Notice that `memories` stays as it is. The bots keep what they learned between rounds.\n\nIn `resetGame`, empty `rivals` and then build the two bots again.",
  "m7/Which one catches you?": "Does the smart bot get better as more memory dots appear? A tally gives you evidence instead of a feeling.\n\nPlay several rounds and tally which bot catches you. Change the balance of smart and simple bots until the game feels good. Then play in a completely new pattern and see what happens to the smart bot.",
  "m8/Accuracy can hide things": "Imagine a model that is right 95% of the time. Now imagine the 5% are nearly all one group of people. A single number hid the problem. A **confusion table** shows what the model guessed against what really happened.\n\nTalk with a partner. Can a model be 95% accurate and still be unfair?",
  "m8/A table in a list of lists": "A **2D array** is a list of lists. Here it makes a table with one row for each real move and one column for each guess. Every count starts at 0.\n\nType this at the top, below `let lastMove`. `MOVES` lists the four moves in the order the table uses:",
  "m8/Count each guess": "`MOVES.indexOf()` turns a word into its place in the list, which is a row or column number. If the word is not in the list, `indexOf` gives -1, so stop there.\n\nWrite a function called `recordGuess(actual, guessed)` at the bottom. It adds 1 to the table cell for that row and column.",
  "m8/Collect the results": "The table only fills up if every guess is recorded. The best moment is when a real move is about to be saved, because then you know the right answer.\n\nRight before you push a new memory, call `recordGuess` with the move that really happened and what `predictMove()` guessed.",
  "m8/Draw the table": "A loop inside a loop visits every cell of the table. The diagonal, where row equals column, is where the guess was right, so those cells are drawn in green.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**, then finish the line marked `TODO` so each cell shows its count.",
  "m8/Who does it fail for?": "The bot learned from you, so it may not predict someone else as well. When a system works worse for some people, that is called **bias**.\n\nPlay, then read your table. Which move does it confuse most? Swap seats and see how it predicts your partner. Then test your Teachable Machine model on people who look different from you, or in different light.",
  "p1/Add falling speed": "To fall like a real thing, the player needs a **velocity**: how fast they move each frame. `VY` stands for velocity in y. **Gravity** is a small number that will change that velocity.\n\nType these two lines below your other variables, after `let playerSize = 40;`:",
  "p1/Make it fall": "Every frame, gravity makes the fall a little faster. Then the fall moves the player down. That is why a falling thing speeds up.\n\nType these lines at the start of `draw()`, right after `background`. Then press **Run** and watch the player speed up as they drop:",
  "p1/Remember if you can jump": "Soon the player needs to know whether they are standing on something. A **boolean** is a variable that can only be `true` or `false`, which fits a yes or no fact.\n\nMake a variable called `onGround` that starts as `false`. Put it below `let gravity = 0.6;`.",
  "p1/Land on the ground": "The player falls straight through the floor. The ground starts at `y = 360`, and the player's feet are at `playerY + playerSize`.\n\nBelow the physics lines, write an `if`. When the feet pass 360, put the player back on top, stop the fall, and set `onGround` to `true`.",
  "p1/Jump!": "p5 runs a special function named `keyPressed()` once each time a key goes down. A jump is one big **negative** velocity, because up means a smaller y. Gravity then slowly brings the player back down.\n\nType this at the very bottom, below `draw()`. It makes the space bar jump, but only when the player is on the ground:",
  "p1/Tune the feel": "Game designers spend a long time on how a jump feels. A small `gravity` feels floaty, like the moon. A big one feels snappy.\n\nChange `gravity` and the jump strength, which is the `-12`, and press **Run** each time. Try a moon level with `gravity` at 0.2. Keep the values you like best.",
  "p2/Run left and right": "`keyPressed()` fires only once per press, so it cannot handle a key that is held. `keyIsDown()` checks every frame whether a key is down right now, so running is smooth.\n\nType this above the `// physics` line. It moves the player 4 dots left or right while an arrow key is held:",
  "p2/Stay on the screen": "The player can run right off the screen. `constrain(value, low, high)` keeps a number inside a range, between a lowest and a highest value.\n\nAfter your running code, use `constrain` on `playerX` so the player can never leave the screen.",
  "p2/Describe a platform": "A platform is a rectangle, so it needs four numbers: where it is (x and y) and how big it is (width and height). Variables with clear names keep them straight.\n\nMake variables named `platX`, `platY`, `platW`, and `platH` at the top. Then draw the platform with `rect`, right after the ground.",
  "p2/Airborne by default": "The player should count as in the air unless something is holding them up. So each frame starts by saying they are **not** on the ground. The ground or the platform will set it back to `true`.\n\nType this line right above `// landing on the ground`:",
  "p2/Land on the platform": "This is the longest condition you have written. `&&` means **and**, so every check must be true. The player must be falling, be over the platform, and have feet at its top.\n\nType this above the `// ground` line. Each check sits on its own line so it is easier to read:",
  "p2/Pick your theme": "This is **your** platformer. Is it a jungle, a castle, or a candy world? The colors set the mood, and a better hero is coming soon.\n\nChange the numbers in `background` and in the `fill` lines for the ground and the platform. Press **Run** to see your theme.",
  "p3/Set up game variables": "A game needs to remember what has happened. `won` and `lost` are booleans that start as `false`, and every part of your game can check them. The other variables hold the score and where the coin, spike, and flag sit.\n\nType these lines at the top, after `let platH = 20;`:",
  "p3/Grab a coin": "`dist(x1, y1, x2, y2)` gives the distance between two points, so it can tell when two things touch. `!` means **not**, so `!coinTaken` is true while the coin is still there.\n\nType this above the `// player` line. It draws the coin, and adds 10 to the score when the player's middle gets close:",
  "p3/Add a spike": "A game needs something to fear. A spike is a triangle sitting on the ground, and touching it sets `lost` to `true`. The check uses a box a little smaller than the triangle, which feels fairer to players.\n\nType this above the `// player` line:",
  "p3/Plant the flag": "Now the game needs an ending. A flag stands at `goalX`. The player wins only if the coin is taken **and** they have reached the flag.\n\nAbove `// player`, draw a flag at `goalX`. Then set `won` to `true` when the coin is taken and the player reaches it.",
  "p3/Show what happened": "The player cannot tell yet that they won or lost. `text` can put words on the screen, and an `if` can show them only when `won` or `lost` is true.\n\nPress **Add the starter code** to show the score. Then finish the lines marked TODO, so the game says when the player wins or loses.",
  "p3/Playtest your level": "A level is good when getting through feels satisfying. The best way to find out is to watch someone else play it.\n\nMove the coin, spike, and flag by changing their numbers at the top, then try to beat it. Ask a neighbor to play with no hints, and watch what they do.",
  "p4/Write a resetGame function": "Right now the only way to replay is to press Run again. A function can fix that. It is a named set of instructions, and this one will put the game back to its start.\n\nAt the bottom, write a function called `resetGame` that puts back every starting value that changes during play.",
  "p4/Press R to restart": "A function does nothing until it is **called**. The player needs a key that calls yours. Inside `keyPressed()`, the variable `key` holds the key that was pressed.\n\nInside `keyPressed()`, add a new `if` that checks whether `key === \"r\"` and calls `resetGame()`.",
  "p4/Your first for loop": "A **for loop** repeats code while it counts. It has three parts: where to start, how long to keep going, and what to change each time. This one starts `x` at 0, keeps going while `x` is under the width, and adds 40 each time.\n\nReplace the ground line `rect(0, 360, 400, 40);` with this loop. It draws a row of tiles:",
  "p4/Loop with math": "The loop's counting variable can do math. With `i` counting 0 to 4, `40 + i * 80` gives each cloud its own `x`. For a zig-zag, add `(i % 2) * 30` to `y`. `%` gives the remainder, so `i % 2` flips between 0 and 1.\n\nRight after `background`, draw a row of 5 clouds with a for loop that counts `i` from 0 to 4.",
  "p4/Your scenery": "Loops are good at scenery, because one shape repeated makes a pattern. Think fence posts, trees, lava bubbles, or castle bricks.\n\nWrite a loop that adds scenery to fit your theme. Then change its numbers, press **Run**, and watch how the pattern changes.",
  "p5/Meet objects": "An **object** bundles related values under one name, like `{ x: 220, y: 260 }`. An **array** is an ordered list inside `[ ]`. Put them together and you get a list of platforms.\n\nReplace your four platform variables, `platX` to `platH`, with this list of three platforms:",
  "p5/Draw them all": "`platforms.length` is how many items the list has. `platforms[0]` is the **first** one, because counting starts at 0. A loop can visit each platform in turn and draw it.\n\nReplace your old line `rect(platX, platY, platW, platH);` with this loop. You read an object's values with a dot, like `p.x`:",
  "p5/Land on any of them": "Your old landing check only knows about one platform, so the game is broken for now. It needs to test every platform in the list.\n\nWrap the landing check in a loop like the one you wrote. Use `p.x`, `p.y`, `p.w`, and `p.h` in place of the old variables.",
  "p5/Add one line, get a platform": "Here is why programmers love arrays. One new line in the list makes a new platform, and all your loops handle it with no other changes.\n\nAdd a fourth platform by writing one more `{ }` line in your array, with `x`, `y`, `w`, and `h`. Then press **Run**.",
  "p5/Design your climb": "A good level is possible but takes some skill. Your jump reaches about 120 dots high, so plan each step up to be smaller than that.\n\nChange the numbers in your `platforms` list to rearrange them into a climb. Press **Run** and test every jump.",
  "p6/Build the coin list": "One coin is not much to chase. A list can hold many coins. A function can **return** a value, which means hand it back, so `makeCoins()` can hand back a fresh list on every restart.\n\nReplace the three coin variables with a list called `coins`. Fill it with a function, `makeCoins()`, that returns at least three `{ x, y }` objects.",
  "p6/Loop backward and remove": "A collected coin should leave the list. `splice(i, 1)` removes 1 item at position `i`. Removing an item slides the rest down one place, so a forward loop would skip the next coin. Looping **backward** avoids that bug.\n\nReplace your whole old `// coin` block with this:",
  "p6/Fix the flag": "`coinTaken` no longer exists, so the flag needs a new rule. You can only win when the list of coins is **empty**. A list knows how many things are in it. Think about how you could ask it.\n\nChange the `if` for your flag so the player wins only when the `coins` list is empty and they reach the flag.",
  "p6/Fix the restart": "Your `resetGame` still mentions `coinTaken`, which is gone. A restart should bring every coin back, and the function you wrote hands back a fresh list.\n\nIn `resetGame`, replace the `coinTaken` line so `coins` gets a fresh list from `makeCoins()`.",
  "p6/Spread them out": "More coins give players a reason to visit your whole level. `coins.length` is how many are still in the list, so it can show players how many remain.\n\nPlace at least **six** coins in `makeCoins()`. Then use `text` and `coins.length` to show a coins-left counter on the screen.",
  "p7/A Coin class": "A **class** is a blueprint for making many objects that all work the same way. A **method** is a function that belongs to an object. This class's constructor takes `x` and `y`. Its `touches()` should return whether the player is within 30 dots.\n\nAt the bottom, write a class called `Coin`. Give it a `show()` method that draws the coin and a `touches()` method.",
  "p7/Draw a level with letters": "Pros paint levels with letters. Each string is a row, and each character is one 40-by-40 tile. `#` is a platform, `c` is a coin, and `.` is empty air. This is **2D** data: a list of rows.\n\nType this at the very top of the file:",
  "p7/Build the world with nested loops": "A loop inside a loop visits every tile. The outer one picks a row, and the inner one walks across it. `level[row][col]` reads a single character. Multiplying by 40 turns tile numbers into pixels.\n\nType this function at the very bottom. It fills `platforms` and `coins` from your letters:",
  "p7/Use the class": "The coins are `Coin` objects now, so each one can draw itself and check itself. You call a method with a dot.\n\nIn your coin loop, replace the `ellipse` line and the `dist` check with calls to `c.show()` and `c.touches()`.",
  "p7/Plug it in": "`buildLevel()` does nothing until it is called. The game needs it once at the start, and again on every restart so the coins come back.\n\nCall `buildLevel()` in `setup()`. Call it again in `resetGame()`, in place of `coins = makeCoins();`.",
  "p7/Paint your own level": "Now level design needs no math. You move characters around and the program builds the world.\n\nChange the letters in `level` to design your own level, then press **Run** and play it. Make sure every coin can be reached.",
  "p8/Add a camera": "A camera is an illusion. The player stays near the middle while the world slides. `translate(x, y)` shifts everything drawn after it. `push()` and `pop()` save and restore that shift, so the score stays pinned to the screen.\n\nAdd these three pieces: the variable under `let onGround = false;`, the camera lines after `background`, and `pop();` above `// score and messages`:",
  "p8/Unlock the right edge": "The player is still stuck inside 400 dots, because `constrain` stops them at both edges. `max(a, b)` gives whichever number is larger, so it can hold one limit only.\n\nReplace your `constrain` line. Keep the left limit at 0 and remove the right one, using `max`.",
  "p8/Follow the ground": "Ground tiles must appear wherever the camera is looking. `floor` rounds a number down. Snapping means rounding a position down to the nearest multiple of 40, the width of one tile.\n\nMake your ground loop start `x` at `cameraX` snapped to a multiple of 40, and run until `cameraX + width`.",
  "p8/The generator algorithm": "An **algorithm** is a precise recipe. This one is a **random walk**: place a platform, take a random step, then repeat. Your jump travels about 160 dots across and 120 up. So every gap must stay under 90 and every height change under 70.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**. Then finish the two lines marked TODO.",
  "p8/Switch to the generator": "The generator replaces the level you drew with letters. Not every coin must be collected now, so the flag should only care about reaching the end.\n\nCall `generateWorld()` everywhere you called `buildLevel()`. Then change the flag's `if` so it only checks that the player reached `goalX`.",
  "p8/Lava below!": "Without danger, a player could walk along the ground and skip every jump. So the ground becomes deadly once they leave the starting area, which ends at 420.\n\nInside your ground landing check, set `lost` to `true` if `playerX` is past 420.",
  "t2/Mix a color": "So far you have used color names. To get **any** color, mix it yourself. Three numbers say how much **red, green, and blue** light to use, each from 0 to 255.\n\nType this line above the body `ellipse`. It makes the monster green:",
  "t2/Add eyes": "The computer runs your code from top to bottom, one line at a time. This is called **sequence**. A shape drawn later paints over the shapes drawn before it, so the dark pupils must come after the white eyes.\n\nType these lines below the body `ellipse`. They draw two white eyes, then two dark pupils on top:",
  "t2/Break the order on purpose": "Does the order of your lines really matter? Test it by drawing the body last.\n\nCut the body `ellipse` line and paste it below the eyes, then press **Run**. The eyes vanish, because the body painted over them. Then move the line back so the eyes show again.",
  "t2/Add a mouth and outlines": "An outline is the line around a shape. `stroke` sets its color, `strokeWeight` sets how thick it is, and `noStroke()` takes it away. `arc` draws part of an oval. Its last two numbers are the angles where it starts and stops, and `PI` is a half circle.\n\nType these lines at the end of `draw()`, above its closing `}`. They draw a red mouth:",
  "t2/Make it yours": "Right now your monster looks like everyone else's. Changing the colors and shapes will fix that. Horns, teeth, spots, or a third eye would all work.\n\nAdd at least **two new features** to your monster, so it looks like nobody else's.",
  "t5/Spot the problem": "A ball that flies away is not much of a game. To keep it on screen, the code must ask a question every frame: has the ball passed the wall?\n\nPress **Run** and watch the ball. It leaves the canvas and never comes back.",
  "t5/Bounce off the right wall": "An `if` lets your code decide. It asks a question, and the lines inside its `{ }` only run when the answer is true. `width` is how wide the canvas is. Multiplying `speedX` by `-1` turns 3 into -3, so the ball moves left.\n\nType these lines below `ballY = ballY + speedY;`. They turn the ball around at the right wall:",
  "t5/All four walls": "The ball bounces off one wall, so three are left. `<` means less than, and `height` is how tall the canvas is. The left wall is where `ballX` is 0, and the top is where `ballY` is 0.\n\nWrite three more `if` checks, one each for the left, bottom, and top walls, so the ball never leaves the canvas.",
  "t5/Fix the sinking edge": "Look closely at the right wall. The ball sinks halfway in, because `ballX` is its **center**. The ball is 40 wide, so its edge is 20 dots ahead of the center.\n\nChange the line `if (ballX > width) {` to this. Now the ball turns around when its edge reaches the wall:",
  "t5/Change color when it bounces": "An `if` can do more than flip a speed. Adding **else** gives it a second set of lines, which run when the answer is false. `height / 2` is halfway down the canvas.\n\nReplace the line `fill(255, 220, 80);` with these lines. The ball turns pink in the bottom half and blue in the top half:",
  "t5/Add a bounce counter": "Every score in every game uses the same trick. A variable starts at 0, and the code adds 1 to it each time something happens. Here that something is a bounce off the right wall.\n\nMake a variable that counts bounces off the right wall. Add 1 to it inside that wall's `if`, and show it with `text`.",
  "t5/Bug hunt": "A **bug** is a mistake that makes code do the wrong thing. This square should turn red when the mouse is on the right half of the canvas. It never does. Remember that the canvas is 400 wide.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**. Then find the wrong number below the line marked TODO and fix it.",
  "t6/Send another star": "Only one star falls, and then nothing happens. The star needs to go back to the top once it passes the bottom. `random(20, 380)` gives a different number between 20 and 380 each time, so the star starts in a new spot.\n\nType these lines at the end of `draw()`, above its closing `}`:",
  "t6/Add a score": "A game needs a score, and the computer has to remember it. That is a job for a variable. `text` then puts the number on the screen, so the player can see it.\n\nType the first line at the top, below `let starY = 0;`. Type the rest at the end of `draw()`:",
  "t6/Did we catch it?": "Checking whether two things touch is called **collision** detection. A catch means the star is low enough, and right of the basket's left side, and left of its right side. `&&` means **and**: every part must be true.\n\nType these lines above `if (starY > height) {`. A catch adds 1 to `score` and sends a new star:",
  "t6/Add lives": "A missed star should cost something. `lives` is a variable that counts down from 3. A star passing the bottom is a miss, so that check is where one life is lost.\n\nAdd these three lines. The first goes below `let score = 0;`. The second goes right below `if (starY > height) {`. The third goes below the `Score` text line:",
  "t6/Game over": "The round should end when lives run out **or** the player reaches 20 points. `||` means **or**: the check is true if either side is true. `noLoop()` stops `draw()` from repeating.\n\nType these lines at the end of `draw()`, above its closing `}`:",
  "t6/Make it get harder": "A good game gets harder as you get better. Right now the star always falls 4 dots each frame. If that number lived in a variable, your code could raise it.\n\nMake a variable named `fallSpeed` that starts at 4. Use it in place of the `4`, and raise it a little on every catch.",
  "t6/Bug hunt": "One `=` **sets** a value. Three, `===`, **compares** two values. Mixing them up causes a bug. `console.log(score);` shows the score below your game, so you can watch what happens to it.\n\nPress **Add the starter code** and catch some stars. Your score gets stuck. Add `console.log(score);` above the `if` marked TODO, then fix that `if`.",
  "c1/Name some code": "You have been calling functions like `rect()` for weeks. Now you will write your own. A **function** is a named set of instructions. You write it once, then run it by typing its name.\n\nReplace the five tree lines in `draw()` with `drawTree();`. Then type the new function at the very bottom of the file:",
  "c1/Give it a parameter": "Calling `drawTree()` twice draws the same tree on top of itself. A **parameter** fixes that. It is a variable in the function's `( )` that the caller fills in. Each call can give a different `x`.\n\nChange your `drawTree` function to match this, with `x` in the `( )` and in the math. Then replace `drawTree();` with the two new calls:",
  "c1/More parameters": "A function can have more than one parameter. A second one named `size` lets you make big trees and small trees. Every measurement is multiplied by `size`, so 1 is normal and 2 is double.\n\nChange your `drawTree` function to match this. Then replace your two calls with these three:",
  "c1/A function that answers": "Some functions do not draw anything. They work out an answer and hand it back. `return` sends that value to the line that called the function. This one turns a size into a shade of green.\n\nType the `leafGreen` function at the very bottom of the file. Then replace `fill(40, 130, 70);` with the new `fill` line:",
  "c1/Grow your forest": "Your scene needs more than trees. A rock, a house, or a cloud could each have its own function, with a parameter that says where it goes.\n\nWrite a second function of your own with at least one parameter, such as `drawRock` or `drawCloud`. Call it a few times in `draw()`.",
  "c1/Bug hunt": "A parameter only helps if the function uses it. Here three bushes should appear in three places, but you only see one. The function gets `x` and never uses it.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**. Then find the number in `drawBush` that should change from bush to bush, and fix it.",
  "c2/Make your sprite": "A **sprite** is a picture used for a character in a game. Yours will replace the rectangle. A second frame with a small change, like a leg or a wing, lets it move later.\n\nOpen **Sprites** above the editor. Draw your hero, or choose one from the library. Use **Duplicate frame** to make a second frame. Name it exactly `hero`, then press **Use in this project**.",
  "c2/Load it before the game starts": "Pictures take time to load. p5 runs a function named `preload()` first and waits for it to finish before `setup()`. Your project's sprites live in a list called `SPRITES`. `loadImage` loads your hero, and the variable `heroImg` holds it.\n\nType these lines above `function setup() {`:",
  "c2/Keep the pixels crisp": "Your sprite is a small picture, and the game stretches it to the player's size. Browsers blur small pictures when they stretch them. `noSmooth()` keeps your pixel art sharp.\n\nType this line in `setup()`, below `createCanvas(400, 400);`:",
  "c2/A drawPlayer function": "`image` draws a picture on the canvas. It works like `rect` with one extra value in front: which picture to draw. Your function skills from the last lesson go to work here.\n\nReplace the line `rect(playerX, playerY, playerSize, playerSize);` with `drawPlayer();`. Then type the new function at the very bottom of the file:",
  "c2/Animate it": "A **sprite sheet** is one picture with the frames side by side. `image` can draw one slice of it. `%` gives what is left over after dividing, so the frame count wraps back to 0. Dividing `frameCount` by 8 changes the frame every 8 frames.\n\nIn `drawPlayer`, replace your `image` line with these four lines:",
  "c2/Check your work": "Good programmers check that nothing broke after a big change. Your player is a picture now, so everything it did before should still work.\n\nPress **Run** and try everything in your game again. If your hero's size feels wrong, change `playerSize` and run it again.",
  "c2/Use it somewhere new": "Your hero is not the only thing that could be a picture. A coin, an enemy, or a door could be one too. You decide what it is and where it goes.\n\nDraw a **second sprite** for something else in your game. Load it in `preload()` the same way, and show it with `image`.",
  "c3/What is a class?": "A **class** is a blueprint for making many objects that work the same way. Its **constructor** runs once to set up each new one. `this` means this particular one, so `this.x` is that critter's own `x`.\n\nType this class at the very bottom of the file:",
  "c3/Teach it a method": "A **method** is a function that belongs to a class. This one, `show()`, draws a critter using its own `this.x`, `this.y`, and `this.size`. Methods do not start with the word `function`.\n\nType these lines inside the class, below the constructor's closing `}`:",
  "c3/Teach it to move": "A second method will move the critter and bounce it off the walls. Each critter uses its own `this.x` and `this.speedX`, so they all move on their own.\n\nType these lines inside the class, below `show()` and above the class's last `}`:",
  "c3/Make a crowd": "`new Critter(x, y)` builds one object from the blueprint. `critters.push` adds it to the end of the list. One loop can do that ten times. Another loop can ask each critter to move and show itself.\n\nType the first loop in `setup()`, below `noStroke();`. Type the second in `draw()`, below `background`:",
  "c3/Click to add": "p5 runs a function named `mousePressed()` once for every click. Here each click adds one critter to the list, at the spot where the mouse is. The loop in `draw()` handles the rest.\n\nType this function at the very bottom of the file:",
  "c3/Your own species": "A **property** is a value that belongs to one object, like `this.size`. If each critter gets its own value, each one can look a little different. It could be a color, a mood, or a number of legs.\n\nGive critters a new property of your choice. Set it in the constructor and use it in `show()`.",
  "c4/One variable to rule them": "A game is always on one screen at a time: the title, the game, or the ending. Which screen it is on is called the game's **state**.\n\nOne variable can remember the state as a word. Yours starts as `\"title\"`, because the title screen comes first.\n\nType this line at the very top of your code:",
  "c4/Rename your game loop": "All of your gameplay lives inside `draw()` right now. Soon `draw()` gets a new job: choosing which screen to show. So the gameplay needs a function name of its own.\n\nYour game will stop working for a moment. That is fine, and the next step fixes it.\n\nFind the line `function draw() {` and replace it with this:",
  "c4/The new draw()": "The new `draw()` is a traffic controller. It checks `gameState` and calls the function for that screen. `===` means \"equals\".\n\n`else if` asks another question only when the `if` before it was false. The last `else` catches anything left over.\n\nType this new function at the very bottom of your code:",
  "c4/A title screen": "`draw()` calls `showTitle()` when the state is `\"title\"`, so now you write that function. It paints a dark background and shows your game's name.\n\n`textAlign(CENTER)` centers text on the `x` you give. The last line switches it back to `LEFT`, so your score still sits where it did.\n\nType this function at the very bottom of your code:",
  "c4/An ending screen": "`draw()` calls `showEnding()` when a round is over, but that function does not exist yet. This one is yours to write. Use `showTitle` as your model.\n\nIt should clear the screen and show the score. Your `won` variable tells you which message to show.\n\nAt the bottom, write a function named `showEnding` that says whether the player won or lost, shows the score, and says to click to play again.",
  "c4/Click to change state": "Nothing changes the state from `\"title\"` to `\"play\"` yet, so the game is stuck on the title. A click should start a round.\n\np5 runs a function named `mousePressed()` once for each click. Calling `resetGame()` first clears the old score and positions.\n\nAt the bottom, write `mousePressed` so a click on any screen except the game calls `resetGame()` and sets the state to `\"play\"`.",
  "c4/Decorate your screens": "A title screen is the first thing a player sees, so make it yours. It can show your hero as well as words.\n\nChange `\"MY GAME\"` to your real title. Then draw your hero on the title screen by adding `image(heroImg, 170, 240, 60, 60)` inside `showTitle`, and press **Run**.",
  "c5/Search for the best": "To find the highest score, walk along the list and remember the best one seen so far. Checking every item in turn like this is called **linear search**.\n\n`findBest` gives back the **index** of the largest score, which is its position in the list. It begins looking from the position named `start`.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**, then finish the line marked TODO so that a bigger score becomes the new best.",
  "c5/Swap two items": "Sorting means trading the places of two scores. That trade is called a **swap**.\n\nA swap needs a spare spot. To swap the drinks in two glasses you would need a third cup. Here `temp` is that cup: it holds one score so it is not lost.\n\nType this helper function at the very bottom of your code:",
  "c5/One sorting step": "Now you can sort. **Selection sort** finds the best score that is left and swaps it to the front. Then it repeats from the next position.\n\n`sortedUpTo` marks how much of the list is finished. Each step finishes one more bar.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**, then finish the three lines marked TODO using `findBest`, `swap`, and `sortedUpTo`.",
  "c5/Watch it run": "Your sort works, but nothing runs it yet. Running one step every half second lets you see each swap. `frameCount % 30 === 0` is true once every 30 frames.\n\nFinished bars turn gold, so you can watch the sorted part grow.\n\nType the first piece after the `background` line in `draw`. Then replace the `fill(120, 170, 255);` line with the second piece and press **Run**:",
  "c5/Count the work": "How much work does sorting take? One way to measure it is to count every time two scores are compared.\n\nA counter inside the `findBest` loop goes up by 1 on each comparison. Showing it on screen lets you watch the work add up.\n\nType the first line after `let sortedUpTo = 0;`. Put the second as the first line inside the `findBest` loop, and the rest at the end of `draw`:",
  "c5/Bug hunt": "Real code has bugs, and finding them is a skill. This function should add up every score. The total should be a number, but it shows `NaN`, which means \"not a number\".\n\n`console.log` prints a value so you can see what the loop is really reading.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**. Follow the TODO to print each value inside the loop, find the one that is wrong, and fix the loop.",
  "c5/Predict, then test": "How fast does the work grow when the list gets longer? Sorting 16 bars took about 120 comparisons. Guessing first makes the answer mean more.\n\nWrite down your guess for 32 bars. Then change the `16` in `setup` to `32` and press **Run**. Make the bars narrower if they do not fit. Were you right?",
  "c5/Use it somewhere new": "Your sort puts the biggest score first. A golf game would want the opposite, because the lowest score wins there.\n\nOnly a small part of your code decides which score counts as \"best\".\n\nChange your sort so the **smallest** score ends up first. Decide which line has to change before you touch anything, then press **Run** to test it.",
  "c6/Name it for real": "Your game is about to be published, so it needs its real name. If your title screen still says MY GAME, now is the time to commit to one.\n\nIn `showTitle`, replace the `text(\"MY GAME\", 200, 160);` line with one like this, using your own game's name:",
  "c6/Add the sparks file": "**Particles** are tiny shapes that burst out, fade, and vanish, like sparks. They make a game feel good to play.\n\nThe hard part is already written for you, in a file of its own. Your game can use its functions without you typing them.\n\nPress **Add the finished file**. Then look at the tabs above your code to find `sparks.js`.",
  "c6/Burst on a score": "Every game in the class is different, but all of them have a `score`. So the sparks watch it. `lastScore` remembers the score from one frame ago.\n\nWhen `score` is bigger than `lastScore`, the player has scored, and `burst` fires the sparks.\n\nType the first line at the very top of your code. Then type the rest at the end of `playGame`:",
  "c6/Make a plan card": "Your game needs **one new feature** that no lesson showed you. Game makers write a **plan** first: what the feature needs, before any code.\n\nPick something small enough to finish today. One good feature beats three broken ones.\n\nPress **Add the starter code**. Then replace each TODO with your plan: what the feature is, what the player does, and which code you will change.",
  "c6/Build your feature": "This is the hardest part of the course, and the best. Nobody is giving you the code this time, so feeling unsure is normal.\n\nSmall pieces are the way through. A shield, a timer, a power-up, or a new enemy are all good ideas.\n\nBuild the feature from your plan. Add one small piece, press **Run**, and check it works before you add the next.",
  "c6/Playtest and fix": "You know your game too well to see what is confusing in it. A **playtest** means watching someone new play it. Real studios do this constantly.\n\nGiving hints would hide the problems, so stay quiet and watch.\n\nSwap seats with someone from a **different road**. Watch them play without helping. Note one thing that confused them, then fix it.",
  "c6/Publish!": "Your game is finished, so it is time to share it. The Arcade is where your class plays each other's games.\n\nPress **Share to Arcade** at the top, so your class can play your game. Then submit this lesson, and go play what your classmates built.",
  "e1/Listen first": "A **sound effect** is a short sound that tells the player something happened. Good ones match the moment: a bright sound for points, a big one for a win.\n\nPress **Open Sounds** and listen to what is there. Pick one sound for scoring points and one for winning.",
  "e1/Play it once": "Your game loop runs 60 times a second. A sound needs a single moment so that it plays **once**.\n\n`scoreBefore` remembers the score from last frame. The frame where `score` is bigger is the moment a point was scored.\n\nType the two variables at the very top of your code. Then type the rest above the `// score and messages` line:",
  "e1/Break it on purpose": "Why does the sound need an `if` around it? Breaking the code on purpose is a good way to find out. Predict what you will hear first.\n\nTake away the `if` line and its closing `}`, so only `playSound(\"coin\");` is left. Press **Run** and listen. Then put the `if` back.",
  "e1/Win and lose": "The end of the game needs a sound too, and it has the same problem. `won` or `lost` stays true on every frame after the game ends.\n\n`overBefore` can remember whether the game was already over last frame, as `scoreBefore` did for the score.\n\nBelow `scoreBefore = score;`, play a win or lose sound **one time** when the game ends. Use `overBefore`, and update it every frame.",
  "e1/A sound for your road": "Sound is **feedback**: it tells players what their action did. Your hero's biggest move deserves a sound of its own.\n\nFind the line where your hero does something big, like jumping, firing, or opening a door. Add a `playSound` there. The **Sounds** button at the top can add the line for you.",
  "e1/Test with sound off": "Some players can't hear your game. Others play with the sound turned off. The game should still make sense to them.\n\nOpen **Display**, turn game sound off, and play your game. Change anything that now only makes sense with sound.",
  "e2/Lay down a beat": "A song starts with a **beat**: a steady pattern of drum hits that the rest of the music follows. The Music Maker shows the beat as a row of numbered steps.\n\nPress **Open Music Maker**. Put a Kick on steps 1 and 9, and a Snare on steps 5 and 13. Then press Play.",
  "e2/Write a melody": "The **melody** is the tune, the notes you would hum. Every note in a Mood fits with the others, so you can experiment freely.\n\nLeave some squares empty, because silence is part of music.\n\nAdd bass, then a melody. Name your song `theme`, and press **Add to my game**.",
  "e2/Bring it in": "Your game needs its song in a file named `theme.js`. Look at the tabs above your code to see if you have one.\n\nIf there is no `theme.js` yet, press **Add the finished file** for a starter tune. You can replace it with your own any time.",
  "e2/Start the music": "The song is in your project, but nothing plays it yet. `playMusic(theme)` starts it.\n\n`resetGame` runs when a new round begins, so it is the right place to start the music.\n\nType this line at the end of `resetGame`, above its closing `}`:",
  "e2/Stop when it is over": "Play until you win or lose and listen. The music keeps going after the game ends, which feels wrong.\n\n`stopMusic()` turns the song off. It should run once, at the moment the game ends.\n\nFind where your code plays the win or lose sound, and stop the music there with `stopMusic()`.",
  "e2/Read your song": "Your song is stored as text, so you can edit it like code. Each word is one step, and `.` is a silent step. `tempo` sets how fast the music plays.\n\nOpen `theme.js` and read your song. Change `tempo`, and change one note. Then press **Run** and listen.",
  "e2/Whose music is it?": "You wrote this song, so you hold its copyright. A **license** is a note that tells others what they may do with your work.\n\nAdd a comment at the top of `theme.js` with your name and a license, like `// Theme by Sam. CC BY 4.0`.",
  "e3/One note": "Browsers only allow sound after a click or a key press. So your first note waits for a click.\n\n`playNote` plays one note. `\"C4\"` is the note's name and `0.3` is how long it lasts. Setting `playing` to true is used later.\n\nType this function at the very bottom of your code. Then press **Run** and click the game:",
  "e3/A note is a number": "Sound is air shaking. A note's **frequency** is how many times the air shakes each second, so every note is really a number. A bigger number is a higher note.\n\nDoubling the number gives the same note, one octave higher.\n\nIn the `playNote` line, change `\"C4\"` to `440` and click the game. Then try `880`, like this:",
  "e3/Pick from a list": "A scale is a list of notes that sound good together. Yours is the `notes` list at the top. Because it is a list, `random` can choose a spot in it.\n\n`frameCount % 15 === 0` is true once every 15 frames, which keeps a steady beat. `heard` remembers each spot that was played.\n\nType this below the `// music goes here` line:",
  "e3/Draw what you hear": "Your `heard` list remembers every note played, so you can draw the melody. Each note becomes a small bar. Notes further along the scale sit higher on the screen.\n\n`heard.shift()` removes the oldest note once the list gets long, so the drawing stays on screen.\n\nType this at the end of `draw()`, above its closing `}`:",
  "e3/A smarter melody": "Random jumps sound messy, because real tunes mostly move to nearby notes. A **random walk** fixes that. It moves up or down a little from the last note.\n\n`constrain` keeps a number between a lowest and a highest value, so the walk stays on the list.\n\nKeep a `walk` variable outside `draw`. Each beat, move it up or down by at most 1, and use it as `spot`.",
  "e3/Which sounds better?": "You now have two algorithms for the same problem. One picks any note at random, and the other walks to a nearby note. Which makes the better tune is for a listener to judge.\n\nHave a neighbor listen to each one with eyes closed. Write down which one they prefer and why.",
  "e3/What does 8-bit mean?": "Computers store sound as a list of numbers. **Bits** set how many different values each number can have. With 8 bits there are only 256 choices.\n\nThis code draws a wave the way the computer would store it. Fewer bits make it look like steps.\n\nType this at the end of `draw()`. Then change `bits` to 1, 2, and 8, and press **Run** each time:",
  // Variables (t3, t4). Students finished these unable to say what a variable is, so each card says
  // one thing about it: it is a name for a value, you use it by name, and something changes it.
  // Where a step brings in a new idea, the instruction names the idea first. It used to sit folded
  // under "Why?", and students typed the code without having met the word for it.
  // Lesson 1 names each command's parts on screen, since nothing has been explained yet.
  // Steps whose first action, or what "it" means, was folded away under Why.
};

for (const [key, brief] of Object.entries(BRIEFS)) {
  const [id, title] = key.split('/');
  const step = getLesson(id)?.steps.find((s) => s.title === title);
  if (!step) throw new Error(`No step for brief: ${key}`);
  step.brief = brief;
}
